::: {.doc-subject}

Find an Eulerian path in the graph if one exists.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L69844-L69968){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.eulerian_path(
    source=None,
    edges=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Eulerian path](../assets/graphs/eulerian-path.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The house of Santa Claus: a square with both diagonals and a roof. Only the bottom corners D and E (outlined) have odd degree, so the Eulerian path runs from one to the other, using each of the eight edges once; edges are numbered in the order the path takes them, and the card lists D, E, C, B, D, C, A, B, E." width="100%"}

An Eulerian path is a trail that uses every edge exactly once (nodes may repeat). It
exists when the edges form one connected piece and, on an undirected graph, either every
node has even degree (the path is then a closed circuit) or exactly two nodes have odd
degree, which must be its two ends. On a directed graph, every node must have as many
edges in as out, except possibly one start node with one extra out-edge and one end
node with one extra in-edge.

This is the classic "draw the figure without lifting your pen" problem, and it appears
in route planning (snow ploughs, mail delivery, street inspection), DNA fragment
assembly (de Bruijn graphs), and circuit design. `is_semieulerian()` and
`is_eulerian()` test for the two cases; `eulerian_circuit()` returns closed trails only.

As in those methods, isolated nodes are ignored.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Where the path should start, as an integer ID or a string label. If the graph only
    has an open path, this must be one of its two possible ends. If `None` (default), a
    valid start is chosen.

<code>[edges]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `False` (default), return the path as node IDs. If `True`, return edge IDs, which
    are unambiguous when the graph has parallel edges.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[int] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The path as node IDs (one more than the number of edges; the first and last are the
    same for a circuit) or, with `edges=True`, edge IDs. `None` if no Eulerian path
    exists (from `source=`, if given).
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `source=` does not exist.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~has_eulerian_path`) 

:   Check if a path exists.

[](`~eulerian_circuit`) 

:   A path that ends where it started.

[](`~enumerate_trails`) 

:   Every trail between two nodes.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a triangle with a tail, only `C` and `D` have an odd number of edges, so the path runs
from one to the other:

::: {#019d6618 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A"), ("C", "D")]
)

[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in graph.eulerian_path(source="D")]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['D', 'C', 'B', 'A', 'C']
```
:::
:::


### Crossing the six bridges

`konigsberg()` is the city of Königsberg as Euler studied it in 1736, with seven bridges
joining its two islands and two river banks. With the Honigbrücke closed, a walk from the
South Bank can cross the other six bridges once each and end on the North Bank. Kneiphof
is joined to each bank by two bridges, so the land masses alone don't say which bridge is
crossed. With `edges=True`, the path gives the bridges:

::: {#673faf47 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
closed = ec.konigsberg().delete_edge("Kneiphof", "Lomse")
label = closed.get_label_from_id
bridge = dict(closed.get_edge_df().select("id", "bridge").iter_rows())
walk = closed.eulerian_path(source="South Bank", edges=True)

(
    [label(n) for n in closed.eulerian_path(source="South Bank")],
    [bridge[e] for e in walk],
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(['South Bank',
  'Kneiphof',
  'North Bank',
  'Kneiphof',
  'South Bank',
  'Lomse',
  'North Bank'],
 ['Grüne Brücke',
  'Krämerbrücke',
  'Schmiedebrücke',
  'Köttelbrücke',
  'Hohe Brücke',
  'Holzbrücke'])
```
:::
:::


